==========================================================
OVERARCHING HYPOTHESIS
==========================================================

IF an adversary attempts to steal, impersonate, reuse, or otherwise abuse
Oracle authentication mechanisms or credential relationships,

THEN Oracle Unified Audit records will contain evidence of abnormal proxy
authentication, identity misuse, unauthorized authentication methods,
unexpected account relationships, or suspicious session activity,

BECAUSE Oracle auditing records authentication methods, identity mappings,
proxy relationships, and account activity required to establish and maintain
authenticated database sessions.

==========================================================
STORY 2A — UNAUTHORIZED PROXY USER IMPERSONATION
==========================================================

Priority
--------
High

Indicators
----------
• New DBPROXY_USERNAME observed.
• One proxy authenticating as many Oracle users.
• Proxy authenticating privileged accounts.
• Proxy authentication from new CLIENT ADDRESS values.
• Proxy authentication from unexpected HOST systems.
• Excessive proxy authentication volume.

Logic

Oracle proxy relationships are generally static and application-specific.
Unauthorized proxy authentication or abnormal impersonation patterns may
indicate application compromise, stolen service account credentials, or
privilege abuse.

Splunk Query
------------
index=database
| rex field=_raw "(?i)DBPROXY_USERNAME[=:]\"?(?<proxy_user>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)CURRENT_USER[=:]\"?(?<db_user>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)CLIENT ADDRESS[=:]\"?(?<src_ip>[0-9A-Fa-f:\.]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)HOST[=:]\"?(?<host>[^\"]+)"
| search proxy_user=*
| stats count dc(db_user) AS impersonated_users values(db_user) AS users values(src_ip) AS source_ips values(host) AS hosts earliest(_time) AS first_seen latest(_time) AS last_seen by proxy_user
| convert ctime(first_seen) ctime(last_seen)
| sort -impersonated_users


Query Logic
-----------
Extracts Oracle proxy authentication activity and identifies proxy accounts
used to authenticate as multiple database users. Proxy accounts impersonating
an unusually large number of users should be validated against expected
application behavior.

Investigation Pivot Fields
--------------------------
• DBPROXY_USERNAME
• CURRENT_USER
• CLIENT ADDRESS
• HOST
• SESSIONID

Operational Notes
-----------------
• Verify whether the proxy account belongs to an approved application.
• Review historical proxy activity.
• Pivot into privilege escalation and object access.
• Review recent application configuration changes.

Expected False Positives
------------------------
• Enterprise middleware.
• Connection pools.
• Oracle Enterprise Manager.
• Approved application testing.

==========================================================
STORY 2B — EXTERNAL IDENTITY MAPPING CHANGES
==========================================================

Priority
--------
High

Indicators
----------
• New EXTERNAL_USERID observed.
• New GLOBAL_USERID observed.
• Multiple enterprise identities mapped to one Oracle account.
• One enterprise identity authenticating multiple Oracle accounts.
• Unexpected AUTHENTICATION_TYPE.
• Authentication from unfamiliar CLIENT ADDRESS.

Logic

Enterprise identities generally remain stable. New identity mappings or
unexpected authentication methods may indicate federation abuse, identity
provider compromise, or unauthorized authentication.

Splunk Query
------------
index=database
| search "LOGON"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)CURRENT_USER[=:]\"?(?<db_user>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)EXTERNAL_USERID[=:]\"?(?<external_user>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)GLOBAL_USERID[=:]\"?(?<global_user>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)AUTHENTICATION_TYPE[=:]\"?(?<authentication_type>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)CLIENT ADDRESS[=:]\"?(?<src_ip>[0-9A-Fa-f:\.]+)"
| stats count values(external_user) AS external_identity values(global_user) AS global_identity values(authentication_type) AS authentication_types values(src_ip) AS source_ips by db_user
| sort -count


Query Logic
-----------
Identifies Oracle accounts associated with enterprise identities and highlights
unexpected identity mappings or authentication methods that differ from normal
usage patterns.

Investigation Pivot Fields
--------------------------
• CURRENT_USER
• EXTERNAL_USERID
• GLOBAL_USERID
• AUTHENTICATION_TYPE
• CLIENT ADDRESS

Operational Notes
-----------------
• Validate the identity mapping.
• Compare against enterprise identity provider records.
• Review authentication history.
• Pivot into subsequent Oracle activity.

Expected False Positives
------------------------
• Identity provider migration.
• PKI certificate renewal.
• New employee onboarding.
• Directory synchronization.

==========================================================
STORY 2C — AUTHENTICATION METHOD ANOMALIES
==========================================================

Priority
--------
Medium

Indicators
----------
• New AUTHENTICATION_TYPE observed.
• Same user authenticating using multiple methods.
• Authentication method changing unexpectedly.
• Privileged accounts using unfamiliar authentication methods.

Logic

Authentication methods generally remain consistent for individual Oracle
accounts. Sudden changes may indicate configuration manipulation, credential
abuse, or attempts to bypass existing authentication controls.

Splunk Query
------------
index=database
| search "LOGON"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)CURRENT_USER[=:]\"?(?<db_user>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)AUTHENTICATION_TYPE[=:]\"?(?<authentication_type>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)CLIENT ADDRESS[=:]\"?(?<src_ip>[^\"]+)"
| stats count dc(authentication_type) AS auth_methods values(authentication_type) AS authentication_methods values(src_ip) AS source_ips by db_user
| where auth_methods>1
| sort -auth_methods


Query Logic
-----------
Identifies Oracle accounts authenticating with multiple authentication methods.
Unexpected authentication method diversity may indicate unauthorized access,
configuration changes, or credential misuse.

Investigation Pivot Fields
--------------------------
• CURRENT_USER
• AUTHENTICATION_TYPE
• CLIENT ADDRESS
• SESSIONID

Operational Notes
-----------------
• Validate approved authentication methods.
• Review authentication history.
• Correlate with identity management changes.

Expected False Positives
------------------------
• Authentication migration.
• PKI rollout.
• MFA implementation.
• Identity provider upgrades.

==========================================================
STORY 2D — MULTIPLE IDENTITIES USING THE SAME DATABASE ACCOUNT
==========================================================

Priority
--------
Medium

Indicators
----------
• Multiple EXTERNAL_USERID values mapped to one Oracle account.
• Multiple GLOBAL_USERID values mapped to one Oracle account.
• Multiple CLIENT ADDRESS values within short periods.
• High authentication diversity.

Logic

Individual Oracle accounts generally have a single associated enterprise
identity. Multiple identities authenticating as the same Oracle account may
indicate account sharing, impersonation, or identity compromise.

Splunk Query
------------
index=database
| search "LOGON"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)CURRENT_USER[=:]\"?(?<db_user>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)EXTERNAL_USERID[=:]\"?(?<external_user>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)GLOBAL_USERID[=:]\"?(?<global_user>[^\"]+)"
| stats dc(external_user) AS external_users dc(global_user) AS global_users values(external_user) AS external_identity values(global_user) AS global_identity by db_user
| where external_users>1 OR global_users>1


Query Logic
-----------
Identifies Oracle accounts associated with multiple enterprise identities.
Unexpected identity diversity may indicate impersonation, shared credentials,
or unauthorized federation.

Investigation Pivot Fields
--------------------------
• CURRENT_USER
• EXTERNAL_USERID
• GLOBAL_USERID

Operational Notes
-----------------
• Validate approved identity mappings.
• Review recent identity changes.
• Correlate with identity provider records.

Expected False Positives
------------------------
• Identity consolidation.
• Federation migration.
• Directory synchronization.

==========================================================
STORY 2E — FIRST-TIME AUTHENTICATION SOURCE
==========================================================

Priority
--------
Medium

Indicators
----------
• New CLIENT ADDRESS.
• New HOST.
• Previously unseen workstation.
• New authentication source for privileged users.

Logic

Threat actors frequently authenticate from infrastructure that has never
previously accessed the Oracle environment. Identifying first-time
authentication sources provides an opportunity to detect compromised accounts
early in the intrusion lifecycle.

Splunk Query
------------
index=database
| search "LOGON"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)CURRENT_USER[=:]\"?(?<db_user>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)CLIENT ADDRESS[=:]\"?(?<src_ip>[^\"]+)"
| rex field=_raw "(?i)HOST[=:]\"?(?<host>[^\"]+)"
| stats earliest(_time) AS first_seen latest(_time) AS last_seen values(host) AS hosts by db_user src_ip
| convert ctime(first_seen) ctime(last_seen)
| sort first_seen


Query Logic
-----------
Establishes the first observed authentication for each Oracle account from a
given CLIENT ADDRESS. Recently observed authentication sources should be
validated to determine whether they represent legitimate administrative
activity or potential account compromise.

Investigation Pivot Fields
--------------------------
• CURRENT_USER
• CLIENT ADDRESS
• HOST
• SESSIONID

Operational Notes
-----------------
• Verify the endpoint with the asset inventory.
• Determine whether the user normally authenticates from the source.
• Pivot into all subsequent Oracle activity from the same session.

Expected False Positives
------------------------
• New workstations.
• VPN changes.
• Device replacement.
• Disaster recovery activities.

==========================================================
DETECTION ENGINEERING OPPORTUNITIES
==========================================================

• Detect first-time DBPROXY_USERNAME usage.
• Detect proxy accounts authenticating unusually large numbers of users.
• Detect new EXTERNAL_USERID or GLOBAL_USERID mappings.
• Detect multiple enterprise identities associated with a single Oracle account.
• Detect Oracle accounts authenticating with multiple AUTHENTICATION_TYPE values.
• Detect first-time CLIENT ADDRESS or HOST authentication for privileged users.
• Detect proxy authentication originating from previously unseen systems.
